# Ceremony budget

Process costs the user's time and tokens. Spend it where a mistake is expensive
or hard to undo, not on every edit. Day-to-day development here works; treat
the existing standards as trusted rather than something to re-prove each turn.

## Default: make the change

One subsystem, no risk trigger below? Edit it, let the `stop` hook run the
owner tests, and report what changed in a few lines. No subagent, no plan
document, no todo list, no deslop suite, no extra review cycle. Stay in
Agent mode; switch to Plan only when the user asked for a plan. Ambiguity
is not a ceremony trigger — pick the reversible default.

`framework plugin stop hook (`framework-workflow-gate`)` runs owner `pytest` and GUI `vitest`
for files this conversation touched under the open project root, under linked
git worktrees of this repo (`~/.cursor/worktrees/<repo>/<name>`), and, when
`project.env` sets `WORKTREE_DIR`, under checkouts in that directory.
That is the verification floor for those roots. Do not spend tokens duplicating
it with a subagent, a full-suite run, or a manual rerun of the same targets.

## Non-trivial work: auto-launch specialists

When the work is more than a small local edit — new capability, unclear
approach, cross-addon/runtime blast radius, or a meaningful diff headed to PR —
**auto-launch** project subagents per `AGENTS.md` without asking the user to
pick or manage them (BA / tech lead / impact / code review / contract /
verify / observe / parity-assess as the matrix says). Advisory reviewers stay
readonly; verify, observe, and parity-assessor may run shell (parity for
foreign inventory only). When launching readonly agents, put diffs/paths in
the Task prompt — do not expect them to run `git`. Parallelize independent
agents. Parent still owns edits and decisions. Hygiene audits stay **skills**,
not agents.

## Root cause over bandaid

Prefer fixing the mechanism that allows a failure (hooks, scripts, harnesses,
validated helpers, contracts) over adding rule sentences, denylists,
read-back theater, or memory-only patches. Guidance text should be a thin
pointer to enforcement, not the enforcement itself.

## Risk triggers that earn ceremony

Escalate only for auth, authorization, secrets, or trust boundaries;
migrations, seeds, or data lifecycle; payload, renderer, or cross-runtime
contract changes; addon manifest, dependency, or removability changes; Docker
or Compose topology; roughly five or more source files or a new subsystem; a
plan the user asked for; or the same check failing twice.

On those triggers, prefer the matching framework evidence agents (impact before
edits; contract + verify after; observe after authorized runtime ops) in
addition to any global BA/tech-lead/code-reviewer launches.

## Escape hatch

`quick`, `just do it`, `no ceremony`, `don't verify`, or similar skips remaining
ceremony on large work for the rest of the session: no subagents, no deslop,
no memory write unless a durable lesson actually appeared, and a report no
longer than the change deserves. Direct is already the default for trivia;
those phrases are not required to start editing.

## Never traded away

Reversibility and honesty are not ceremony. Stop for destructive irreversible
data loss, never claim a check ran when it did not, and never disable or
weaken a failing test to end a turn.
